Z||000004||Will||11-20-2000||03:34 PM||||LowCG:<BR>     That's an interesting idea. I don't know what the TDC cam specs are, however.<P>87GTZ34:<BR>     Could you please post what your manual says about lift duration, etc.?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||11-20-2000||08:12 PM||||I got looking at a '93 Lumina shop manual today and was both amused and impressed by the TDC 3.4.<BR>     What struck me as most important, however, is that the cams are inserted from the front the SAME way that an in-block camshaft is inserted, unlike the N* cams, which are held in by removable saddles.<BR>     What are the specs for the 3.4 pushrod cams? Journal/bore diameters etc.?<BR>     Is it possible that the cams for the 3.4 TDC are cut from the same blanks as the cams for the 3.4 pushrod?<BR>     If they are, then an aftermarket cam grinder could most likely grind a set to your sepcifications.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||87GTZ34||11-20-2000||09:50 PM||||Sorry Will, I just saw your post.  I have spoken to Brian Crower to see what they could do for me but I need to send him some hardware for the next step.  What grind are you after? I'm going to put a turbo on mine so I don't want anything radical.<P>'the book' reads intake  .370 in @ 115 BTDC<BR>                 exhaust .370 in @ 108 ATDC<BR>I would speculate (and i shouldn't do that) the cams are not dialed in based on the cam timing procedure.  'the book' lists +/- 2 deg per cam and they don't have to all be the same offset. who knows maybe w/ a hot engine using belt drives its a mute point...<P>Ron||||reg||1
Z||000007||87GTZ34||11-20-2000||09:55 PM||||I forgot to mention that high lift is probably out of the question due to spring coil bind.  I have notes on the springs that I found from Crower but I need to compile them into something meaningful, but I recall that there wasn't much room left when the valve was open. I don't know what other valves are available yet and if any are, do they have extra clearance in the cam carrier.<P>Ron||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||11-21-2000||12:32 AM||||I don't actually have one, I just think it's a really cool engine: a factory DOHC job built on a pushrod block. I get a kick out of that.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><BR>     I have been musing for a little while now about building an 8000+ RPM 275++ fwhp TDC sometime in the future.<BR>     I'd probably have to have cylinder head in my hands to inspect, but it might be possible to relieve the spring seat somewhat and use longer springs with the same nubmer of coils so that the valve could lift further without binding. How much would 24 custom double wound springs cost? Who knows, there might be a Honda spring that's exactly what would be needed.<BR>     I couldn't tell from the pictures in the manual; does a TDC use litteraly the same block as the pushrod, or just the same casting but without the lifter bores cut?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||msweldon||11-21-2000||10:00 AM||||waaahoooo,<P>finally some people interested in finding about head & cam work for the TDC.<P>My neighbor, 1FST2M6 may be able to find out about alternate springs, e.g. honda from a local shop he is a partner of.<P>As soon as I get my shop manuals from helm I'll try to help out with this thread as much as possible.<P>let's keep this thread going.<P>marc||||reg||1
Z||000010||FIEROCIOUS||11-21-2000||12:18 PM||||Um,...Why don't you just get the cams re-ground? They can make the base circle smaller so that the differance between closed and open are more drastic and change the duration and centerline as well. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||msweldon||11-21-2000||12:58 PM||||FIEROCIOUS:<P>I've pondered the same thing and Eglin Cams mentioned that that may be a possibility, the only sticky point is whether the lifters could take up the slack from the grind.<P>The other issue is to find a stronger replacement spring for the valves due to the stock springs starting to float just above 7000 rpm.<P>marc||||reg||1
