Z||000013||87GTZ34||11-21-2000||02:29 PM||||Will - sounds like you're thinkin the same thing I'm thinkin... Can you say all aluminum 3.4 DOHC!<P>I don't think the lifters will take up enough slack so the valve/spring height would need to be increased. If that can be done the lifter would need to be checked for bind/gouge. And if all that works... hang on and keep new belt on it!<P>free spring height    1.6551in<BR>load pressure closed  65 lbs @ 1.4in<BR>load pressure open   160 lbs @ 1.030in<BR>'the book' doesn't list id & od so I will have to measure when I tear it down unless someone has the info or has their's apart.||||reg||1
Z||000014||msweldon||11-21-2000||02:40 PM||||If a ground cam necessitated a valve/spring height increase, that may be a problem locating aftermarket valves for the 3.4 dohc, cause there aren't any in existance as far as I know.<P>hmmm...custom wound springs, special machined valves and a custom ground cam, ouch...this is getting expensive.<P>time to track down those engineers of the dohc and see if this sucker really did put out the 275hp, and how...<P>marc||||reg||1
Z||000015||Will||11-21-2000||05:36 PM||||Its obvious from looking at the shop manual that GM was trying to save as much money as possible designing this engine.<BR>     Could the TDC use the same (dimensionally, at least) valves, retainers, and keepers as the pushrod engine?<BR>     The same valve springs, even? The pushrod engine probably can't go to 7k, but the lighter valve train of the TDC might let the same springs wind to 7k.||||reg||1
Z||000016||terryk||11-21-2000||06:03 PM||||I had my 2.8L X-11 engine to 7k several times. There was 3k in the engine alone, (balanced and had dang good springs, carbed/short runners, good cam, blah, blah). The problems was *I* couldn't handle it. It sounded fine, but I squirmed doing it. The DOHC's start to float out around 7-7.5K too. They do sound nice up there though!||||reg||1
Z||000017||FIEROCIOUS||11-21-2000||11:36 PM||||What does the rocker arm set up look like is it a fixed rocker or is an adjustable set up? little screws on the valve side? If it is fixed then a smaller base circle cam wont work but if it it adjustable then the possibility is there....Too bad it doesn't use conventional ie; SBC rockers then all you'd need to do is increase the duration then use higher ratio rockers....just a few thoughts I had... ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Shaun41178||11-22-2000||04:36 AM||||I don't have any specific information to add but mostly hearsay.<P>I have spoken to Dave Rodabaugh who said that he has done tests on the valve sizes, intake and exhaust ports, and cam size of the 3.4 DOHC and he told me that the cams for the DOHC motors have more then enough lift and duration then what the ports and valves can actually flow.  He says that by going to larger cams will not give more power.  Now I have not done these tests and I also have not dissected this and done the math but Dave is a mechanical engineer and if he says that he punched in all the numbers and calculations and that was the result then I am going to take his word for it.<P>He says that to get more power from the DOHC the intake runners, ports, valves, exhaust ports and exhaust manifolds will have to be modified to get more power.  He said the easiest way was to work with the exhaust and specifically the exhaust manifolds.  He was running a computer program with length and diameter of headers on a computer program and with a certain setup on the exhaust his program told him that the engine would make about 250 hp.  I beleive he said that was with just headers but don't quote me.<P>I don't understand why noone has talked about adjustable cam gears.  It seems to me that this would be one of the first things people would want to play with since modifying the intake and exhaust cams separate from each other have been known to give good power increases on 4 cylinder hondas.  I would think a good 10 hp is hidden there somewhere or perhaps even more.  Now I bet adjustable cam gears aren't made for the DOHC but I bet it can't be more expensive to have those made then to have custom springs and custom cams made.<P>What about PROM's?  Noone has mentioned proms yet.  I would think that by playing with the computer and somehow advancing spark and timing curves, an increase in hp can be seen there as well.  I mean my goodness with 4 cams and 4 valves per cylinder spark timing and cam timing has to play a big role in power output.  I am not an expert on the DOHC or really know if the 280 hp rumors are true but I am sure that 280 hp can be achieved from that engine without bigger cams.<P>My suggestions, listen to Dave and play with some kind of header design that is larger then the stock setup, get adj cam gears, and try to find a PROM or some kind of computer reprogrammer to change the spark and advance curves.  I think that will give around 280 and still rev to 7k+<P>I thought Dave was a member of Pennocks and would have responded to this topic by now but I guess not.  I might have to email him to make sure he gives you guys all the details.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>2.8 With nitrous<BR>Soon to have a 3.2 with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000019||1FST2M6||11-22-2000||08:14 AM||||i'll go look at the shop and see what will match... the GS-R spring is the same dimension not sure about spring rate though... I'll let ya know...  and Ferrera makes valves for the car.. they make valves for EVERYTHING!<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 11-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||terryk||11-22-2000||11:55 AM||||There are a few of us playing with cam timing and eproms. By design, the cam timing is adjustable. <P>So far, the upper end can be improved but it tends to dog the bottom end. And, once again, the GM tuning of the eprom is turning out to be pretty dang good. <P>Dyno testing hasn't given *any* indication that 280HP is possible. Maybe with 11:1 compression, bigger ports, more lift, blah, blah.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 11-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||Will||11-22-2000||12:17 PM||||FIEROCIOUS:<P>     There are no rocker arms. The engine uses direct acting hydraulic tappets.||||reg||1
Z||000022||FIEROCIOUS||11-22-2000||12:35 PM||||That is what I thought,...A thought on the cam gears; you ca adjust valve overlap and cam timeing only basicly with cam gears all you can do is adjust your powerband with no real significant increase in HP. <P>Now a thought on the port design and exhast; <BR>What shaun said sounds to be correct, modern manufacturers (dometic) have been limiting power output by port design in an attemt to keep volocity in the intake port up. ie. engine pulls high vacume. whereas imports have limited flow by cam design... You could always mill the head, gain some compression, which in turn will retard the timeing then advance the timeing to correct it and gain a few ponies.||||reg||1
Z||000023||msweldon||11-22-2000||01:06 PM||||I could be wrong on this but, there is the thought you could also increase the compression by using the Northstar pistons and rods from 9.25:1 to 10.25:1 but that would negate any large amounts of boost through additional forced induction.<P>I'm going to attempt to call the "Chevy Buff Resource Center" to get any additional info about the dohc.<P>Terry:<BR>What do you think is ultimately possible in tuning the GM eprom? Is there anything an aftermarket ECM could do, e.g. Haltech, that tuning the GM ecm could not?||||reg||1
Z||000024||87GTZ34||11-22-2000||01:16 PM||||As Terry mentioned in his post & I in a previous post in this thread, the cam timing on the DOHC is adjustable by design.  CMD's procedure for service is speed (not accuracy) so I would have to believe getting the current settings dialed in would be a power improvement.  I have no desire to change lift & duration for this 4 valve engine as VE should be pretty good already. All I want is stonger valve hardware a quicker springs to go to 7.5K rpm. Anyone with real info please email me & I can be the guiney pig.  I plan on having the head worked by a shop that specializes in 4 valve engines and then turbo charging it.  If low-end and spoolup is a problem, then on goes the NOS.  For those of you planning not to enhance induction, I've heard that N* 10 & 1/4:1 pistons fit.  I need to go the other way on mine though so I won't have any experience there for y'all.<P>Sounds like someone should track the progress of upgrades for this engine so I will put a spreadsheet together.  Email me if you want a copy.  <BR>There is a fella that is GP crazy that did some work on the Z34.  His page is <A HREF="http://members.home.net/gtp/." TARGET=_blank>http://members.home.net/gtp/.</A> <P>Ron<P>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 11-22-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 11-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||msweldon||11-22-2000||01:25 PM||||I've already started a real simple spreadsheet of all the "possible" upgrades to the dohc.  Its nothing anyone who is really interested in the dohc hasn't probably seen already.<P>As I probably will not start my install until early next year, I will not have any "real" upgrade numbers for a short time after.<P>If anyone want's the spreadsheet just email me.<P>marc||||reg||1
Z||000026||terryk||11-22-2000||05:17 PM||||Msweldon,<P>I'm not sure what you mean but everything can be tuned I guess. Spark, ESC, VE, PE, AE, auto shift points, etc. <P>The main work I did was to figure out the spark and fuel and get rid of the limiters. A guy wanted to take it to 7000+ in his Fiero. I have one Diacom run a 7850. I thought it was fine, but he felt the valves were floating. We haven't done much on the spark yet because it's pulling some timing so bumping it up more will just pull even more timing. I think it can take more above 5000. We'll see. <P>A second DOHC Fiero should be running this weekend. I thought he might get it running sooner. Then I can really see what it will do with a bit less fuel and more spark.||||reg||1
Z||000027||msweldon||11-22-2000||06:45 PM||||Terry:<P>Was anything special done to the TDC with the 7800 rpm run, besides the removal of the rev limiter, e.g. springs, valves, porting, etc....<P>THanks,<BR>marc||||reg||1
